From 6c6cc9765e2236ec083545dedc4af59a72fdd2a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 14:35:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 6/9] hv: vmbus: retain buffer ownership until GPADL and
 page state are resolved

Converting rings and host-visible buffers to the chunked allocator
introduces a lifetime window that the previous contiguous allocation
never had. A buffer can now be mapped into userspace through UIO while
its GPADL is still being established, and a channel can be rescinded
while a create or teardown message is in flight. Freeing the backing
pages at the wrong point either leaks them to the host or hands pages
back to the allocator that are still host-visible or still decrypted.

Track each buffer with a channel-keyed retained owner that records the
allocation address, its chunk and page arrays, and the encryption state
that was actually reached. Owners move to a delayed reclaimer only after
three gates clear: the GPADL is resolved, the page state is known, and
the per-page references taken by UIO mappings have returned to their
baseline. A create or teardown with an uncertain outcome retains the
owner rather than freeing, so a partial post can never release pages the
host may still map.

The shared-page test that 2/9 grew is pulled into
vmbus_needs_shared_pages() here, so the three inputs -- Hyper-V
isolation, guest-memory encryption through CC_ATTR_GUEST_MEM_ENCRYPT,
and the per-buffer confidential flag -- are named at the call site
instead of composed inline. That is also what lets KUnit pin the
private-versus-shared selection without a live host. See 2/9 for why
the condition is not a bare hv_is_isolation_supported() test.

Distinguish host rescind from local unload, since both previously set
the same channel flag and a rescind-based free could drop pages after a
successful-looking teardown that left its GPADL handle set. Revocation
now goes through an explicit channel-keyed entry point, and the
disconnected VMBus message path frees its allocated context.

In-tree migrated callers use descriptor-aware _owned entry points. The
previously exported allocator, free, GPADL establish, caller-decrypted
establish and teardown signatures are preserved through adapters. The
compat establish still decrypts a range whose owner did not, and the
compat teardown re-encrypts that range before returning, exactly as
this symbol always has: its callers free the memory themselves as soon
as it returns, so there is no owner left to defer the work to reclaim.
The owned path defers the same work through owner->raw_decrypted. This
is also where vmbus_alloc_buffer() gets its four-argument prototype
back: 1/9 temporarily widened it with a bool confidential argument,
which now lives only on vmbus_alloc_buffer_owned().

A UIO mmap races the reclaimer in the other direction. The page array
and the pages behind it can be freed between reading them and building
the mapping, so vmbus_buffer_pin_pages() snapshots the array and takes
a reference on every page under the owners lock. The reclaimer already
refuses to free a folio whose reference count is above one and refuses
to begin once it has started reclaiming, so the references close the
window without adding a second lifetime. vmbus_buffer_unpin_pages()
drops them once the VMA holds its own.

The reclaimer workqueue is torn down by cancelling every timer-pending
delayed_work before destroy_workqueue(): drain_workqueue() only drains
work that is queued or running, and a delayed_work still waiting on its
timer is not on the workqueue yet. An owner that is already ready to
reclaim is queued once more so that drain frees it before the
workqueue goes away. The shutdown walk uses cancel_delayed_work()
rather than the _sync variant under the owners lock, because reclaim
work that is already running blocks on that same lock and
destroy_workqueue() waits for it afterwards. A mapping that still holds
pages at that point cannot be freed safely, so those pages are retained
and flagged rather than dropped under a live mapping.

The owner records two distinct encryption facts. needs_encrypt is set
when the chunked allocator decrypted pages for a shared mapping and
they must be re-encrypted before release; reclaim does that per chunk
through page_address(), which is the direct map that
set_memory_encrypted() requires. raw_decrypted covers the legacy
whole-range case, where a consumer established a GPADL over memory it
decrypted itself and set gpadl.decrypted on the public struct
vmbus_buffer. No in-tree caller takes that path today -- every in-tree
establish passes memory_prepared=true -- but the struct is public and
vmbus_release_buffer() is exported, so the field is part of the
external surface and stays.

KUnit coverage exercises the reclaim gate, the reclaim schedule gate,
host-revoke state, mapping references and repeated owner release.

Suggested-by: Michael Kelley <mhklinux@outlook.com>
Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
